<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Devon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Devon is a county of south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> with coasts on both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> Channel. Its name comes from the Dumnonii, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> people of the peninsula, through the post-Roman kingdom of Dumnonia. The area was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> in the 7th and 8th centuries, and its county town is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/devon.jpg" alt="Devon" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neil Theasby · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dartmoor_ponies_by_the_B3212_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4546147.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wessex was a farming kingdom of chalk downland, fertile river valleys and, in the west, the heavier lands of Somerset and <b>Devon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Other parts of the island kept Celtic languages: Gaelic, and perhaps some Pictish, in most of Scotland, Cornish in Cornwall and parts of <b>Devon</b>, Cumbric perhaps to the 12th century in parts of Cumbria, and Welsh in Wales.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Tin from Cornwall and <b>Devon</b> was used to make bronze from about 2200 BCE, and Britain became the first region of Europe to switch all its metalwork to tin-bronze, in 2200–2100 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/bell-beaker-culture">Bell Beaker culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Lyte was a curate in County Wexford from 1815 to 1818, where he often preached at the church in Killurin and came under the influence of its rector, the Reverend Abraham Swanne, which stayed with him; he was later vicar of All Saints' Church in Brixham, <b>Devon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/abide-with-me">Abide with Me</a></li><li><blockquote>Migrants from Dumnonia in south-west Britain, the land of modern <b>Devon</b> and Cornwall, settled the northern coast of Armorica in the 5th and 6th centuries and gave it the name of their homeland.</blockquote><a href="/state/domnonee">Domnonée</a></li><li><blockquote>The name survived in the post-Roman Kingdom of Dumnonia and in the county name <b>Devon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dumnonii">Dumnonii</a></li></ul></section>
